#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157073 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/id #uuid "af260af2-fd05-54bc-8f6f-a5612cb922db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7048" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation ![](images/a8/a8ea187557f2a8104688a946913572d8cd62b579c7d24dd91e4cafbfb61f9874.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](images/b2/b2e09fc2bc82576d702ebc07440760f80b8db1e3607888e469c3661c25ad3371.svg)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](images/8a/8a19755a74fe3d8c582c2408c2d802411fce6449d0e9c00bac00de8d8ca91826.svg)\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/1b/1b43b7c5f8459588632917502c96bb792a86a9f8e6c3a2d67eda71c6ce56f496.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157074 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/id #uuid "f1eaa2b8-544c-5217-82a5-8ec0a311c5a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7049" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n![](images/d6/d66051007ab78d2727f3b501156878a369a04b24bca337ed587feb07935a1de7.svg)\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n![](images/bf/bfd809b436c24efcb2e9934b1c36293dfecb4bf87ab41c05f8cbbb9d506ea34d.svg)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/7e/7e191da3b082da420622418962e7491a6e78b6ed8192259b9b50ec8040bbc525.svg)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/fb/fb833263882adc2422083c111e96b0e030263f93e4c0174082f0f57667b46e8e.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/fe/fe33e849a0e3baa7e8547c9559f9c36e980b8e715cba6e26900eea5d815e9b30.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/2c/2c443847a5559737066873593b399117c03e9c8141b403bc7f820c9ad493e2a9.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](images/00/00b635d3858d38c41ad9e1bacb00ed4c6370b5e2112aa82faa6ef37a83209a6c.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/d1/d1911a797bfa5915fb1478c91dc007ca3f1419b1093180dfb08932a008337b6e.svg)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/id #uuid "38f7a261-b58c-5ce1-ab77-6bfb5ce40b19" :question/math-academy-id "e-7050" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/be/be4b1ac82a25533e28028ad0aade71e2dd2614c5cd302d777a90a9f16be845c1.svg)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/df/dfddfc4e82f21cd490aff75bbdc6fbece98cd05a5cc70a1148492697faa8741a.svg)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/92/92c6c6be816658e065ebba1079a2c7fa031cc9137ffa8dec7bf6e498e69195f3.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/75/75524e426e894f50985740367c96f128dfef1e216ae6969a529889cf22803bc7.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/bb/bb892c2fd7b1a2873b9a7427d95599f67fbd10e47ec6fd2cb377b9f6f45f4185.svg)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](images/46/4696fe4ee3fd46a639fb465c10aa2ac44e608a0cf59ca6c1a210eb4861b3dd5b.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/05/0525d47967c203832ff4deea0931e007cc7127f24d248cd8265765ad95708367.svg)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/48/48da1d81a447aa78c3ffa68ad5f089bc0d8e4bac5fa489ddad2747055b0f2a99.svg) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1635bde-7b58-54e7-895f-915db0b50cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2+t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300848} {:answer/id #uuid "baf6c3b6-ca24-5611-96b8-993adf0129e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300898} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fd8283-591d-5f67-b3cd-17968f78cdfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300945} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a4780e-6ce1-5e3e-8873-06f09e1e4957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301080} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4364a0-440b-5051-a349-f5a8feb58d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -(1+2t){e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4fd8283-591d-5f67-b3cd-17968f78cdfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300945} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52d6758-b968-51b9-a5a2-6e03ab7af674" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d755d77-3b2c-5d58-97ac-ef5c72049848" :question/math-academy-id "q-325135" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4-4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{{c}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(1-t){e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0422cb11-aa21-5804-a176-05ed17cba41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-1.7t}\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299947} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4d7c35-78bf-5342-a653-ea1a2987432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300020} {:answer/id #uuid "31004e1e-f5f2-5c84-942a-fc521ec76c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300199} {:answer/id #uuid "c77c5882-e733-5d04-8246-4ccb1fd85e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300960} {:answer/id #uuid "d6deeab2-dfc5-59e3-b2ac-2f29662a0e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c77c5882-e733-5d04-8246-4ccb1fd85e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300960} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2d80b8-7823-5a2c-a4eb-3fe09645cb4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5878b428-f5a6-5ebd-8385-5b72ac276f64" :question/math-academy-id "q-326150" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.7\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1.7t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d04ce85-c364-52ba-aa30-ca47e307496e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Complete Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9a8c81-a091-545d-84fc-e0dd993ec18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345621} {:answer/id #uuid "434dca2b-bb1e-5b6a-a0fc-484d397d614c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345932} {:answer/id #uuid "581d572d-6189-5f30-8179-ad07a3ead556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346102} {:answer/id #uuid "6c04748c-785f-5be6-8f54-c3dd0b0f7a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346288} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0a53e5-676f-5cb8-9cd4-65e20571d1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}\\,{c}_{1}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581d572d-6189-5f30-8179-ad07a3ead556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346102} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1be337-4b04-5f96-942e-67689c075869" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1a7a31a-a1ff-52db-b87c-67b3aaf6bbd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80548" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dfffefc-7dc4-503a-9b1f-a84d08149c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413826} {:answer/id #uuid "672f41ac-64c5-58b5-868f-7c1ad340ab36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413827} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa05af9-3949-5d3f-84eb-7ccc92942416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413828} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7ac799-30a1-5efa-892d-6a6142f8760c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb961936-d8ed-5956-ad00-aabdf66df6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc7ac799-30a1-5efa-892d-6a6142f8760c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413829} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eaed666-f3ff-5e76-bdcf-86e2bff8289c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9318e8aa-b34c-5ef4-bb58-533fb20cecad" :question/math-academy-id "q-80606" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2}=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "109cc543-253b-5f18-b13b-e54b99e8b12a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "130d3616-3670-5a5c-b8da-00c9c23fef02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345514} {:answer/id #uuid "163b9c8a-95d6-5825-8c03-376d7d06d2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345547} {:answer/id #uuid "21ddf266-a502-52e7-9722-392dfd92faaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1+2t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345652} {:answer/id #uuid "3615743c-9a59-5828-a5f9-76c3c49cabeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2t+1){e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345819} {:answer/id #uuid "7345187f-4860-54a2-b08f-2475beaa1d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7345187f-4860-54a2-b08f-2475beaa1d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346369} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1a6eed3-c8a4-5660-aaac-2d37375ee8a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "608190d5-e5cd-5f82-85c0-726eb6ec9bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-80608" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1-1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}+4{w}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-2{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}-([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c19f9d9-fa4b-5261-b9a0-99a203aeac30" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations in a Defective Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f60048-7d7c-5687-9ef2-9d7a40e3cd6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348782} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce3934e-26ad-50c3-b8ad-8eb9aeefecd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348840} {:answer/id #uuid "34571999-a224-5304-b6f7-661d75ad824f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348917} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5c0860-4e19-5581-8cad-9596b9de6b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349840} {:answer/id #uuid "c2004d97-ccfe-5f1f-a183-c402f97fd670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c5c0860-4e19-5581-8cad-9596b9de6b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349840} :answer-field/id #uuid "35dc67a9-2544-5970-b757-6ab8a5b07cd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef21bab9-f763-5bd8-b177-dccd5650b9c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80918" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =5.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7-5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}]}
